{"name":"Storm Petrel","hex":"#7f95a5","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"storm petrel","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of several small seabirds, of the families Hydrobatidae (northern storm petrels) or Oceanitidae (southern storm petrels), having dark plumage and a white rump."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/storm_petrel"}],"entries":[{"title":"Storm petrel","blocks":[{"text":"Storm petrel or stormy petrel may refer to one of two seabird families in the order Procellariiformes, which were historically treated as one family. Before the introduction of molecular phylogenetics, the traditional arrangement was to divide the Procellariiformes into a set of four families: Diomedeidae containing the albatrosses, Hydrobatidae containing all the storm petrels, Pelecanoididae containing the diving petrels and Procellariidae containing the petrels, shearwaters and fulmars. The family Hydrobatidae was further divided into two subfamilies, the northern storm petrels in Hydrobatinae and the southern or austral storm petrels in Oceanitinae."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c1/European_Storm_Petrel_From_The_Crossley_ID_Guide_Eastern_Birds.jpg/330px-European_Storm_Petrel_From_The_Crossley_ID_Guide_Eastern_Birds.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Storm_petrel","wikidata":"Q29032629"},{"title":"Storm","blocks":[{"text":"A storm is any disturbed state of the natural environment or the atmosphere of an astronomical body. It may be marked by significant disruptions to normal conditions such as strong wind, tornadoes, hail, thunder and lightning, heavy precipitation, heavy freezing rain, strong winds, wind transporting some substance through the atmosphere such as in a dust storm, among other forms of severe weather."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e3/Cloud_cumulonimbus_at_baltic_sea%281%29.jpg/330px-Cloud_cumulonimbus_at_baltic_sea%281%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Storm","wikidata":"Q81054"},{"title":"Procellariiformes","blocks":[{"text":"Procellariiformes is an order of seabirds that comprises four families; the albatrosses, the petrels and shearwaters, and two families of storm petrels. Formerly called Tubinares and still called tubenoses in English, Procellariiformes are often referred to collectively as the petrels, a term that has been applied to all members of the order, or more commonly all the families except the albatrosses. They are almost exclusively pelagic, and have a cosmopolitan distribution across the world's oceans, with the highest diversity being the seas around New Zealand."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/Thalassarche_bulleri_in_flight_3_-_SE_Tasmania.jpg/330px-Thalassarche_bulleri_in_flight_3_-_SE_Tasmania.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Procellariiformes","wikidata":"Q21685"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/14/Oceanites_oceanicus_-_SE_Tasmania.jpg/500px-Oceanites_oceanicus_-_SE_Tasmania.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/14/Oceanites_oceanicus_-_SE_Tasmania.jpg/1280px-Oceanites_oceanicus_-_SE_Tasmania.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/14/Oceanites_oceanicus_-_SE_Tasmania.jpg/500px-Oceanites_oceanicus_-_SE_Tasmania.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AOceanites_oceanicus_-_SE_Tasmania.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4d/Classic_Storm.jpg/500px-Classic_Storm.jpg?utm_